          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Iron deficiency anemia sits in the blood chapter. Which chapter would an underlying bleeding ulcer be coded from?",
          "answer": "**Blood chapter:** Yes. Iron deficiency anemia is D50, the first code in the nutritional anemia block (D50-D53) of the blood chapter, which covers D50 to D89 (source: *ICD-10-CM Chapter 3: blood, blood-forming organs and the immune mechanism*).\n\n**Ulcer chapter:** A bleeding peptic ulcer belongs to the digestive system chapter, which covers K00 to K95 (source: *ICD-10-CM Chapter 11: diseases of the digestive system*). The peptic ulcer categories sit in the K20-K31 block, divided by site. That passage did not list the K25-K28 codes or say how bleeding is captured, so I can't confirm those details from my sources.\n\n**The convention for ulcer-related anemia:** My search for the sequencing rule failed, so I can only give partial support. The blood chapter notes that anemia is often a manifestation of another condition. Its categories carry sequencing instructions that point to other chapters. It also states that chronic blood loss causing iron deficiency is coded from whichever chapter owns the bleeding site. That would point to the digestive chapter for a bleeding ulcer. I could not confirm the exact order of codes or whether the ulcer code is listed first.\n\nFor the exact sequencing rule, check the full ICD-10-CM tabular list or ask a medical coder. Your question was about coding, so this is not a substitute for a clinician's assessment of any symptoms.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
